00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:51.150
And it was the most influential,
uh, because of how, just think of

00:07:51.155 --> 00:07:55.730
the fact that we had three authors
like Dante, Petrarca and Boccaccio.

00:07:56.715 --> 00:08:00.915
All, you know, being born very
close to each other in the span

00:08:00.915 --> 00:08:05.865
of, I think it was less than a
hundred years, or even less maybe.

00:08:05.865 --> 00:08:11.025
So, uh, it was, maybe it was,
there was even a little bit of a...

00:08:11.775 --> 00:08:13.995
like, it was even a coincidence in a way.

00:08:13.995 --> 00:08:18.284
But, uh, a lot of great writers
were born in, in Tuscany, and, and

00:08:18.284 --> 00:08:22.905
that led to Tuscan and Florentine
becoming the, the most influential

00:08:22.905 --> 00:08:25.365
literary, literary language in Italy.

00:08:25.365 --> 00:08:33.564
And, uh, not the only one, but you
know, uh, the one that mostly I, I,

00:08:33.914 --> 00:08:43.395
by the 16th century, uh, became the
accepted, you know, uh, literary and,

00:08:43.395 --> 00:08:49.575
um, sort of the Lingua Franca of, of the
elites in, in cultural elites in Italy.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.960
Of course one of the one attractions
